ZTEXT00100000c5413c3061c0000000
Zero/Ezero 0.11 native tools - Linux x86-64 and Windows x64

Extract the platform toolkit and keep lib beside its executables. The binaries
and libraries are our own Zero implementations. No Bash/CMD/Python script is
needed to run the compiler, preparation tool or local updater.

Compile a source file (Windows appends.exe to the tool name):
  zero examples/hello.zero NEW_PROGRAM
  ezero examples/banana.ezero NEW_PROGRAM
The optional third argument chooses a compiler target; the default is native
Linux ELF or Windows PE for the current host. Existing output files are kept
and cause an error. Run the produced program using the host's normal executable
launch mechanism. Ezero file operations belong to native generated programs;
Zero OS guest apps deliberately report that host files are unavailable.

Prepare a dedicated directory:
  zero-setup prepare zero.iso NEW_DIRECTORY
  zero-setup status DIRECTORY
  zero-setup remove DIRECTORY
Preparation copies the ISO and creates an original empty ZeroFS data file.
The original v1 preparation/removal contract remains compatible.

Deploy a bootable UEFI x64 application and its sibling ISO:
  zero-setup deploy zero.iso ZEROX64.EFI NEW_DIRECTORY
The toolkit includes our own ZEROX64.EFI and its complete Zero source. Firmware
must be able to read the destination filesystem and launch that file; normally
this means FAT. No boot entry or partition is changed automatically. Arbitrary
NTFS/ext4/Btrfs directories and a live storage.data block device remain further
work. v2 status accepts changed valid ZeroFS data; remove still preserves it
by refusal. See installer/README.ztext and installer/boot/README.ztext.

Local staged rolling-release backend:
  zero-update init zero.iso 1000 NEW_STORE
  zero-update publish next-zero.iso 1001 NEW_INBOX
  zero-update check NEW_INBOX NEW_STORE
  zero-update verify NEW_STORE
  zero-update rollback NEW_STORE
  zero-update watch NEW_INBOX NEW_STORE 3600
  zero-update inspect NEW_STORE
  zero-update recover NEW_STORE RECOVERED_STORE
watch repeats local checks while the native process is running, using real
timed waits. An optional final check count makes it finite. A missing or
corrupt inbox stops with an error. inspect exposes incomplete journal tails;
recover creates a new verified store without altering the original. This is
not a registered startup task, network updater or boot activation mechanism. Images
have SHA256 corruption checks, but no publisher signatures or configured remote
release server. Keep previous known-good media. See updater/README.ztext.
Native payload and journal writes request file flushing before publication
or successful commit. Directory metadata is not flushed by the current API;
complete rename/power-loss durability is not claimed.

Verification boundaries: Linux runs natively on Debian13.6; Arch tests use an
official Arch userspace container sharing the host kernel. Windows PE tools
are executed through Wine10 on Debian, not a native Windows installation.
Wine does not emulate all Windows reparse behavior; documented skipped checks
are not claimed as passes. OS boot tests use four QEMU BIOS/UEFI32/64 modes.
